    "result_text": "Build passes green.\n\nDone. To reject a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date with a 400, I followed the existing project idiom with minimal structural impact:\n\n- **`BuildOwner`** (a single-method class, so zero surrounding complexity): added one `else if` branch that throws when the supplied date is after `LocalDate.now()`, plus a `throws` clause. The default-date logic is untouched.\n- **`RegistrationDateInFutureException`** \u2014 new checked exception (mirrors `InvalidPostcodeException`).\n- **`RegistrationDateInFutureExceptionHandler`** \u2014 new handler returning 400 via `ProblemDetails`.\n- **Escalation YAML** wiring the exception to its handler.\n\nThe check sits in `BuildOwner` before the default is applied, so only an explicitly-supplied future date is rejected; an absent date still defaults to today. All tests, including the new `errorRejectsFutureDate` acceptance test, are green.",
